// Lore for the Ghur map. See src/data/lore/index.js.
//
// Every point here is a label on the published Ghurish Heartlands plate, so
// the entries describe what the map itself shows: a realm of mawpaths and
// gutforts where the ground is a predator, the weather is a predator, and the
// only settlements that last are the ones built to be defended. Where Games
// Workshop’s lore runs thin the writing stays with the cartography rather than
// inventing events or people to fill the gap.
export const GHUR_LORE = {
  ghur: `The Realm of Beasts, and the only one of the eight that is honestly named. Ghur does not have wildernesses between its civilised parts; it has civilised parts clinging to the inside of a wilderness that is still eating. Mountains migrate, coastlines are chewed rather than eroded, and the great roads across it are mawpaths — tracks beaten flat by things large enough to beat a road flat.

The Ghurish Heartlands are the realm’s central mass: Thondia at the centre, Rondhol and Lendu west across the Bitingsea, Gallet and Andtor south and east, the frozen sprawl of Bjarl and the Drontal tundra below. Everything worth holding sits on a coast, a crossing or a pass, because those are the only places a garrison can see what is coming.

Sigmar’s cities are here in force and none of them are comfortable. Excelsis is the prize; everything else on this map is a road to it, a threat to it, or a reason someone else wants it first.`,

  // --- Rondhol and the north-western coast --------------------------------
  'river-pierce': `A fast, cold river cutting west out of the Rondhol uplands, named for what it does to the ground rather than for anyone who found it. It is the northern limit of easy movement in western Rondhol: an army that wants to cross does it at the fords or not at all.`,

  vanderhal: `A holding on the open plain of northern Rondhol, far enough from the coast that nothing reaches it by sea and far enough from the passes that nothing reaches it quickly by land. That isolation is the whole of its defence and the whole of its problem — help arrives late here, if it arrives.`,

  'clatchs-war': `A battlefield, marked on the plate the way Ghurish cartographers mark them: crossed weapons and a name, with no date and no account of who won. What is left is broken ground below the Rondhol ridge that grazing beasts still avoid.`,

  'river-bouldergant': `A broad, boulder-choked river draining the western Rondhol plain. The stones in it are large enough to walk on and are moved every season by the floods, so no ford here stays a ford for long, and no bridge built across it has yet outlasted a spring.`,

  rondhol: `The great western region of the Heartlands: dry amber plain rising to broken uplands, bounded north and east by the Bitingsea. It is grazing country, which in Ghur means it is hunting country, and the herds that cross it are followed by everything that eats herds.

Rondhol has no single capital. It has ruins — Tuskvault, Civilia’s Ruin, Brinnock — and the plate names more of its dead settlements than its living ones. Anyone campaigning here is walking over the last people who tried.`,

  'rondhol-heartlands': `The settled core of Rondhol, such as it is: the belt of plain between the ruins of Tuskvault and the coast where the grass is deep enough to support herds year-round. Every faction that wants to feed an army in the west wants this ground, and no one has held it long enough to fence it.`,

  'hartgulper-mawpath': `A mawpath running north-east across Rondhol toward the Mountains of Maraz. Mawpaths are not built; they are the flattened record of something enormous going the same way every year, and armies use them because the alternative is breaking a column through unbroken scrub.`,

  'blazing-crests': `A ridge of red rock on the northern Rondhol coast that catches the light and holds heat long after dark. It is the first landmark ships raise coming south out of the Crashing Gulf, and the first high ground an invader out of the north would have to take.`,

  'drakatoas-gouge': `A long trench torn across the north Rondhol uplands, wide enough to swallow a column and deep enough that the bottom of it is its own weather. The plate gives it a name and no explanation, which in Ghur usually means the thing that made it is still somewhere on the map.`,

  'duel-of-gods': `A battle-site in the northern hills, marked with crossed weapons like any other fight and named for the scale of the combatants rather than the outcome. The ground is scarred in a pattern nothing mortal makes, and it has never grown back.`,

  'mountains-of-maraz': `The dark massif standing over northern Rondhol, the highest ground on the western side of the Bitingsea. The passes through it are few and the plate marks none of them, which tells an invader most of what they need to know about campaigning here in force.`,

  'the-burying': `A marked site on the Rondhol plain south of the mountains, its name doing all the work the plate needs. Something was put in the ground here on a scale that earned a place on a realm map, and the ground has been treated with respect ever since.`,

  'gaping-portal': `A hole in the Rondhol uplands, drawn on the plate as an opening rather than a settlement. Whether it is a realmgate, a cave system or a wound in the realm, it is an entrance onto a map where entrances are worth armies, and it sits astride the only easy line from the coast to the Rondhol Heartlands.`,

  tuskvault: `A ruined stronghold above the Bitingsea coast, built to hold something worth vaulting and no longer holding anything. The walls are substantial enough that a force willing to clear them has a ready-made position on the best ground in eastern Rondhol.`,

  'civilias-ruin': `A dead settlement on the Rondhol shore of the Bitingsea, close enough to Tuskvault that the two of them probably fell in the same season. It keeps a usable landing and a defensible rise, which is why it keeps getting occupied by people who have no intention of rebuilding it.`,

  'amber-steppes': `The open amber grassland of the west, running from the Rondhol plain down into Lendu — the ground that gives Ghur its colour on every map anyone has drawn of it. Nothing about the steppes is defensible; they are crossed, grazed and fought over, and whoever holds the water at their edges controls them without ever having to stand on them.`,

  'diamondhack-rapids': `White water on the river cutting south out of Rondhol into Lendu, named for the way the rock shatters under it. The rapids are impassable to boats and awkward to ford, which makes the crossings above and below them worth more than the river itself.`,

  'thunderbellow-mawpath': `The mawpath running south-east out of Rondhol toward the Bitingsea coast, one of the two great tracks linking the western plain to the water. Anything moving between Beastgrave and the sea uses it, which is reason enough to watch it.`,

  thinnercliff: `A thin headland on the Bitingsea, all cliff and no shelter, standing out into the water between the Crashing Gulf and the Svlind. It has no harbour and no use except the view, and the view is of every keel heading south.`,

  'the-svlind': `A broken stretch of coast south of Thinnercliff where the land shreds into inlets and stacks. Nothing large lands here, which is exactly why small things do.`,

  'daggertooth-coast': `The jagged eastern shore of the Bitingsea, named for the rocks that make it. It is a coast that takes ships rather than receiving them, and the wrecks along it are the closest thing western Ghur has to a trade record.`,

  // --- The Crashing Gulf and the Bitingsea --------------------------------
  'crashing-gulf': `The northern sea of the Heartlands, open, cold and badly behaved. It separates Rondhol from the Thondian shore and it does not do so gently — the crossing is short in distance and long in casualties, and armies that can go round usually do.`,

  'mangrel-isle': `An island in the Crashing Gulf with a shape the cartographer bothered to draw and a name nobody bothered to explain. It sits on the only sensible line between the Rondhol coast and the Thondian one, so whoever is using that line is using this rock.`,

  'the-ravenids': `A scatter of islets at the northern limit of the plate, the last land before the Gulf stops being charted. They are nesting ground, watch-ground and, for anything hiding a fleet, very good ground indeed.`,

  'vensoth-bay': `A deep bay on the north Thondian coast, sheltered enough to hold ships and far enough from Izalend to hold them unnoticed. It is the obvious place to put an army ashore if the intention is to be inland before anyone in Thondia hears about it.`,

  bitingsea: `The inland sea dividing Rondhol and Lendu from Thondia and Gallet, and the single most important piece of geography on this map. Every western campaign is really an argument about who can cross it and who can stop the crossing.

It is called the Bitingsea because of what lives in it. The coasts on both sides are named for teeth — Daggertooth, Mawbight, Fisher’s Gauntlet — and the shipping that survives it does so by being fast, small and lucky.`,

  'colossus-shoal': `Shallow water out in the Bitingsea where something the size of a hill lies under the surface, marked on the plate as a hazard rather than a landmark. Keels that clear it save days; keels that do not are not recovered.`,

  mawbight: `The open eastern reach of the Bitingsea, between the Lendu coast and the Gallet shore. It is the widest water on the map and the crossing everything else on the Bitingsea is measured against.`,

  'lynxs-crescent': `A curved arm of coast reaching into the Bitingsea south of Brinnock, sheltering the water behind it. The crescent is the only piece of that shore a fleet can lie up against, and it is the reason Veldtwend is where it is.`,

  'fishers-gauntlet': `The narrows between the Lendu shore and the southern islands, where the Bitingsea drains into the Sea of Serpents. Everything going south through it goes slowly and in single file, which makes the Gauntlet the best place on the map to lose a fleet and the best place to take one.`,

  // --- Western Thondia ----------------------------------------------------
  'the-stamping': `A marked battle-site in the high country of north-western Thondia, drawn with the crossed weapons the plate reserves for fights worth remembering. The name says how it was won.`,

  'the-mar': `A belt of sour ground across western Thondia — neither plain nor marsh, and useful for neither grazing nor building. Armies cross it because the alternatives are the mountains or the sea.`,

  'twinhorn-peak': `A mountain that used to have two horns. What is left is a shattered stump standing over western Thondia, still high enough to see the Bitingsea from, and still the best observation post between the Mar and the Gnarlwood.`,

  'ursrichts-kill': `A marked site in the shadow of the broken peak, named for the kill rather than the killer. The plate does not say which of them is buried here.`,

  talaxis: `A town in the western Thondian hills, sitting where the high ground gives out and the Gnarlwood begins. It is far enough inland to be safe from the sea and close enough to the forest to be nervous about it.`,

  dogtown: `A settlement below Talaxis, named with the bluntness of a place that grew rather than being founded. It sits on the road between the western hills and the Gnarlwood, and takes its living from everything that has to pass through.`,

  'the-craw': `A marked site on the western Thondian coast where the land narrows to a throat between the sea and the hills. Everything moving along that shore goes through it, which is the whole of its significance and quite enough.`,

  'the-gnarlwood': `A forest in western Thondia dense and old enough to be drawn as terrain rather than scenery. Ghurish woods are not passive: the Gnarlwood closes behind a column, and the tracks through it on one campaign are not there for the next.`,

  'bloodgullet-mawpath': `The mawpath running south-east out of the Gnarlwood toward the Gallet coast, one of the few reliable ways through the forest belt. Its name is a description of the traffic, not a warning.`,

  // --- Thondia ------------------------------------------------------------
  thondia: `The central continent of the Ghurish Heartlands and the oldest inhabited ground in the realm. It is a country of high steppe ringed by the Krondspine mountains, drained toward Lake Everglut, and crossed by mawpaths broad enough to march an army down.

Thondia is where Ghur’s major powers actually touch. Izalend and the Great Excelsis Road run through it, the ogor mawtribes migrate across it, and the ground itself has form for waking up. Nothing here is neutral terrain.

For a campaign map it is the centre of gravity: the passes, the lake, the road and the two skols all sit inside it, and an army that owns Thondia can reach almost anywhere else on the plate.`,

  'the-drench': `The wet northern edge of Thondia, where the Crashing Gulf works its way inland through a maze of channels and standing water. It is bad going for anything heavy and excellent going for anything that wants to arrive unannounced.`,

  'maw-of-ghur': `The realm’s appetite given a place on the map. Ghur is a realm that digests what it holds, and the Maw is where that is least metaphorical — a mouth in the world that the Thondian plain drains toward, feared by the mawtribes who otherwise fear very little.

There is no garrison here and no reason to build one. What makes it worth fighting over is proximity: the Maw sits on the open northern Thondian steppe between the Drench and the Great Gutfort, astride the ground any northern advance has to cross, and armies that camp near it do so because of where it is rather than what it is.`,

  'thunderscorn-peaks': `The mountains at Thondia’s north-eastern corner, where the storms that come in off the Clawing Sea break against the range and stay. The passes are the landward approach to Izalend and they are held by weather as much as by anyone claiming them.`,

  izalend: `Thondia’s northern port, on the Clawing Sea under the Thunderscorn Peaks — a walled harbour town that exists because everything arriving in northern Thondia by water has to arrive somewhere.

It is the far end of the Great Excelsis Road, which makes it the point where the realm’s sea traffic becomes the realm’s land traffic. Hold Izalend and the whole northern approach to Thondia is a supply line; lose it and an army in the north is living off the country, which in Ghur means the country is living off the army.`,

  'great-gutfort': `A fortress on the open Thondian steppe, raised in the way this realm builds: heavy, low and stocked to outlast anything that surrounds it. A gutfort is provisioned to be besieged as a matter of course rather than as a contingency.

It sits in the middle of the northern plain with nothing to anchor it but its own walls, which means it can only be taken by assault and can only be held by an army willing to stay inside it.`,

  'meatfist-mawpath': `The western mawpath into Thondia, carrying the migration traffic between the Thondian steppe and the Bitingsea coast. It is the shortest line from the sea to the plain and it is beaten wide enough to use as a road.`,

  'krondspine-range': `The mountain arc enclosing central Thondia, curving from the north-east around the head of Lake Everglut. It is the wall that makes Thondia a basin, and the passes through it are the reason Krondskol and the Breakface Mawpath matter.`,

  'great-excelsis-road': `The road from Izalend south-east toward Excelsis, the only stretch of engineered highway the plate bothers to name. It exists because the alternative — moving the produce of the northern coast across Thondia by mawpath — costs more than building a road did.

Every raid on the Thondian interior is measured by how long it closes this road for.`,

  'lake-everglut': `The lake at the bottom of the Thondian basin, ringed by the Krondspine mountains and fed by everything the range sheds. Its name is not an exaggeration: the water level rises and falls without regard to season, and the plate draws a shoreline that no one who has camped on it believes.`,

  'glossom-crevasse': `A split in the eastern Thondian ground between the Krondspine arc and the Morruk Hills. It is a barrier to movement rather than a destination, and it forces the traffic between Thondia and Excelsis onto the Breakface Mawpath.`,

  'ymnogs-trample-north': `The northern half of a mawpath running the length of the map, wide enough and old enough to be named for whatever first flattened it. It crosses Thondia’s western edge and carries the traffic between the Bitingsea coast and the southern regions.`,

  vexothskol: `A stronghold on the southern Thondian plain, one of a pair of skols guarding the ground below the Krondspine range. It is built for the same conditions as the Great Gutfort and to the same philosophy: walls, stores, and no expectation of relief.`,

  krondskol: `The eastern skol, standing under the Krondspine range it takes its name from, holding the ground where the passes come down onto the Thondian plain. Anything coming out of those mountains arrives in front of it.

Of all the fixed positions in Thondia this is the one worth the most to a campaign: it controls a pass, sits on the open plain, and cannot be bypassed by anything that intends to keep a supply line.`,

  'breakface-mawpath': `The mawpath running east out of Thondia past the Morruk Hills toward Excelsis. It is the overland road for anything too heavy or too unwelcome to use the Great Excelsis Road, and the two of them are the only ways east that do not involve the sea.`,

  'stone-nautilor': `A vast fossil shell on the southern Thondian plain, large enough to be a landmark and solid enough to be a position. Armies camp inside it for the same reason they camp in ruins, and it is drier than the ground around it.`,

  'morruk-hills': `The broken hill country between Thondia and Excelsis, the last high ground before the coast. It shelters the Breakface Mawpath and hides anything that wants to watch the road without being seen from it.`,

  'rampage-of-kragnos': `A line of destruction across southern Thondia, named for what made it and drawn on the plate as a scar rather than a place. Kragnos went through here; the plate records the route the way it records a river.

There is nothing to garrison. There is a great deal to explain why the country between the skols and the crevasse looks the way it does.`,

  'draktoa-crevasse': `A deep split in the southern Thondian ground, one of a family of gouges and crevasses this plate names across the western half of the realm. It divides the Thondian plain from the Carcass Donse uplands and is crossed in very few places.`,

  // --- Carcass Donse ------------------------------------------------------
  'carcass-donse': `The upland belt between Thondia and Andtor, dry, broken and named for what the ground is made of. Bone weathers slowly in this climate, and the mesas and ridges here are geology with a history.

It is poor country to live in and useful country to cross, which is why the traffic between the northern plain and the southern jungles bends through it.`,

  'nautil-peaks': `A dark massif at the western end of Carcass Donse, rising straight off the plain. The peaks are the western anchor of the upland belt and the reason the road between Thondia and the south runs where it does.`,

  'lost-mesas': `Flat-topped remnants standing out of the Carcass Donse uplands, isolated from each other and from anything else. Their tops are defensible and waterless, which has made them excellent last stands and poor settlements.`,

  'anvil-of-hashut': `A forge worked in the Carcass Donse uplands, sunk into the rock below the bone country where the heat is free and the fuel is closer than it is anywhere else on this coast. It produces iron, and iron is the scarcest thing in a realm this far from Chamon.

Whatever is struck here is worth more than the ground it stands on, which is the only reason anyone garrisons a position this far from water.`,

  // --- Korrok and the Clawing Sea -----------------------------------------
  korrok: `The north-eastern region of the Heartlands, a dry inland plain rimmed by mountains and edged by the Clawing Sea. It is the hinterland of Excelsis — the ground the city cannot afford to lose and has never quite controlled.`,

  'gobspit-mawpath': `The mawpath at the far north-eastern edge of the plate, carrying traffic in and out of Korrok from beyond the map. Whatever comes into the Heartlands from that direction comes down this track.`,

  icefangs: `Ice standing out of the Clawing Sea off the northern Korrok coast, permanent enough to be charted. It is a hazard to shipping and, in the season the floes join up, a road for anything prepared to walk on it.`,

  'clawing-sea': `The north-eastern sea, between Thondia’s Thunderscorn coast and the shore of Korrok. It is the water Izalend faces, the water Excelsis trades across, and it is called the Clawing Sea for the same reason everything else here has teeth in its name.`,

  'brinesalt-winds': `A named wind rather than a place: the salt-laden air that comes off the Clawing Sea and crosses inland over Korrok. It strips the ground it blows over, which is why the Korrok plain is bare and why the caravans time their runs by it.`,

  'coast-of-tusks': `The Korrok shore north of Excelsis, named for the bone spurs standing out of it. This is the coast the city was founded on, and it is the reason there is a harbour here at all.`,

  'wendeas-gorge': `A gorge cutting through the eastern Korrok uplands, deep enough to be an obstacle and narrow enough to be held. It is one of the very few ways through that country for a body of troops.`,

  'hornid-cape': `The eastern cape of Korrok, standing out into the water beyond the gorge. It is the turning point for anything sailing round the north-east of the Heartlands, and the last shelter before the open coast.`,

  bilgeport: `The working harbour on the Korrok coast north of Excelsis — a port named by the people who unload in it rather than by anyone selling passage. Everything Excelsis eats that does not come down the Great Excelsis Road comes through here.`,

  'salzagors-hope': `A settlement on the Korrok shore between Bilgeport and Excelsis, the sort of optimistic foundation that gets named for the founder and then outlives him. It sits on the coast road and takes a cut of everything that uses it.`,

  'spear-of-maelus': `The shard driven into the Korrok coast that Excelsis is built around — a piece of a shattered world, standing out of the water and the rock beside the city. It is the reason the city exists on this otherwise unremarkable shore.

Everything Excelsis is worth comes out of it, and everything that besieges Excelsis is really besieging this.`,

  excelsis: `The great city of Ghur: walls, docks and towers on the Coast of Tusks, built up around the Spear of Maelus and grown into the largest concentration of Sigmar’s people in the Realm of Beasts. It is the administrative centre, the port of entry and the only place on this map that would be recognisable as a city anywhere else.

What makes it a prize is the Spear. The prophecy-stones it yields are the closest thing the Mortal Realms have to foreknowledge, which means Excelsis does not merely trade — it knows things, and everyone who wants to know them has to come here or take the city.

It has been besieged more often than it has been at peace. The walls are heavy, the garrison is permanent, and the plate around it is thick with the names of places that exist to watch the approaches: Amberstone Watch, Bilgeport, Salzagor’s Hope, the Great Excelsis Road. No campaign on this map ends anywhere else.`,

  'amberstone-watch': `The fortified outpost immediately inland of Excelsis, watching the roads from the Morruk Hills and the Drowned Lands. It exists so the city has warning, and a garrison there buys Excelsis the time to shut its gates.`,

  'questing-serpent-river': `The river running south out of the Korrok plain below Excelsis, winding enough to earn its name. It waters the ground the city feeds on and bounds the approach from Carcass Donse.`,

  'the-drowned-lands': `Flooded country east of Excelsis where the Korrok plain gives out into standing water. Nothing heavy crosses it and nothing at all crosses it quickly, which makes it the city’s eastern wall as surely as the masonry is its western one.`,

  'salen-plains': `Open ground east of the Drowned Lands, dry and level and empty. It is the assembly area for anything intending to come at Excelsis from the east, and it is watched accordingly.`,

  'the-pinch': `A narrows in the eastern Korrok country where two arms of water almost meet. Everything moving north or south along that coast squeezes through it, and a small force sitting in it can stop a large one.`,

  'krastels-drowning': `A pale flooded basin south-east of Excelsis, named for whoever was in it when it filled. It is impassable, permanent, and the reason the traffic between Korrok and Andtor bends around its northern shore.`,

  'drogrukh-uplands': `High dry ground in the south-east of Korrok, above the Drowned Lands and below the Krakensea coast. It is the only good going between Excelsis and the eastern seaboard, which makes it the route and the risk at once.`,

  'seabiter-mawpath': `The mawpath along the far eastern edge of the plate, running down to the coast. It is the way in and out of the Heartlands from the east, and the last named track before the map ends.`,

  // --- Lendu and the south-west -------------------------------------------
  beastgrave: `A mountain that hunts. Beastgrave is not a place with beasts in it; it is the beast, a living peak that draws the hungry and the ambitious into its passages and digests them at its own pace.

The plate marks it as ruined, which on this map means broken rather than empty. Whatever was done to it, the mountain is still standing over western Lendu and it still sits astride the only high ground between the Rondhol plain and the southern veldt.

It is the most famous place on this half of the map and the one with the least to plunder. Armies come for it anyway, because in Ghur a thing that eats warriors is understood as a thing worth beating.`,

  brinnock: `A ruined settlement on the Lendu shore of the Bitingsea, with a usable landing and no one left to deny it. It is the obvious bridgehead for anything crossing from Rondhol, which is presumably how it came to be ruined.`,

  veldtwend: `A settlement where the Lendu veldt bends down to the Bitingsea, sheltered behind Lynx’s Crescent. It is the only living port on this stretch of coast and it trades on that fact for everything it is worth.`,

  lendu: `The south-western region: open amber veldt running from the Bitingsea down to the Sea of Serpents, with Beastgrave standing over its northern end. It is grazing country turned campaigning country, flat enough to march across and empty enough that an army is visible for a day before it arrives.

Lendu’s settlements are coastal or ruined, and mostly both.`,

  'neoths-beard': `A tangle of scrub and broken rock on the Lendu coast, thick enough to be drawn as terrain. It masks the shore behind it and is the only cover on that stretch for anything moving toward Veldtwend.`,

  'wailing-pike': `A spire of rock standing over the southern Lendu coast, named for the sound the wind makes through it. It is a navigation mark from the sea and an observation post from the land.`,

  'zaraks-last-charge': `A battle-site on the western Lendu plain, marked with crossed weapons and named for how it ended. The plate does not say what Zarak charged, only that it was the last thing he did.`,

  'the-harm': `A marked site on the Lendu plain east of Zarak’s ground. The name is the whole record.`,

  'neckslit-talon': `A hooked spur of land reaching into the Sea of Serpents from the Lendu shore. It shelters the water inside its curve, which is worth more on this coast than the ground itself.`,

  'great-cleaving-river': `The river splitting the southern Lendu veldt, running west to the sea. It is the boundary between the grazing country and the broken ground below it, and the crossings on it are the only reason Everquake City was ever built.`,

  'lair-of-dathuselai': `A den marked on the southern Lendu coast, given a name of its own on a realm map — which means whatever lives in it is a strategic fact rather than a local nuisance. It sits beside the river mouth and the shipping that uses it.`,

  skavenskrol: `A marked site in the broken ground of southern Lendu, where the burrows under the veldt reach the surface. What comes out of it does not hold territory in any way an army would recognise, which makes it impossible to besiege and impossible to ignore.`,

  'lendus-claw': `The hooked southern tip of Lendu, reaching into the Sea of Serpents. It is the last land on this side of the map and the western marker of the southern crossing.`,

  'everquake-city': `A ruined city on the southern Lendu plain, built on ground that would not stop moving and abandoned when the ground won. The walls are still there in pieces, which in this country makes it a position rather than a memorial.

Anyone campaigning in southern Lendu ends up here, because it is the only masonry for a hundred miles.`,

  'greathind-veldt': `The southernmost grazing country of Lendu, at the western edge of the plate where the map stops and the herds do not. It is the reservoir the whole Lendu economy draws on, and the only thing that makes the region worth holding.`,

  // --- The Sea of Serpents ------------------------------------------------
  'sea-of-serpents': `The southern sea, between Lendu and the ice of Bjarl. It is named accurately. Crossing it is the fastest way from the western regions to the southern ones and the reason that route is not used more often.`,

  shivermouth: `The channel where the Bitingsea drains south into the Sea of Serpents past the Lendu coast. Cold water meets colder here, and the fogs that come out of it cover the southern approach for weeks at a time.`,

  'the-krakens-bite': `A marked site on the southern Lendu coast where something took a piece out of the shoreline. It is charted as a hazard and used as a landmark, which is the usual career of a disaster in this realm.`,

  'gnarwhorl-point': `The southern point of the Lendu coast, spiralled and stacked where the sea has worked at it. It is the turning mark for anything rounding the region from the west, and the last shelter before the open southern water.`,

  // --- Gallet -------------------------------------------------------------
  gallet: `The southern region of the central landmass: red plains running from the Bitingsea coast down to the Splitneck Sea and the ice beyond it. It is warmer, wetter and better settled than Lendu, with real ports on its western shore and real roads across it.

Gallet is the corridor between the Bitingsea and the south. Everything moving between Thondia and Bjarl crosses it, and the Holdbrawl Mawpath is the reason it can be crossed at all.`,

  'templia-beasthall': `A temple on the northern Gallet plain, built as a hall for beasts rather than for worshippers — Ghur’s religion is practised on the thing itself. It is substantial, permanent and garrisoned, which on this plate makes it a fortress with a congregation.`,

  'brenndts-last-fight': `A battle-site on the northern Gallet plain beside the Beasthall, marked with crossed weapons. The proximity is probably not a coincidence.`,

  lentock: `A settlement on the Gallet shore of the Bitingsea, sitting above the water on the only rise for some distance. It is the northern of the region’s two coastal towns and the one that faces the crossing.`,

  'holdbrawl-mawpath': `The mawpath running south-east across Gallet from the coast toward the Godswallow Delta. It is the region’s main road and the only reliable line between the Bitingsea ports and the south.`,

  wreathport: `The working port of southern Gallet, on the Bitingsea below Lentock. It handles what comes off the Mawbight crossing and is the place an army landing in Gallet would want on the first day.`,

  'the-fused-lands': `Ground south of Wreathport where the rock has been run together into a single mass. Nothing grows on it, nothing digs into it, and it is the hardest going in Gallet — which also makes it the only part of the region that cannot be undermined.`,

  'broken-hoof': `A marked site on the Gallet coast at the mouth of the Fisher’s Gauntlet, named the way this map names its disasters. It overlooks the narrows and anything that intends to pass them.`,

  'godswallow-delta': `The delta where Gallet’s rivers empty into the Splitneck Sea, a fan of channels and silt wide enough to be a region in its own right. It is fertile, indefensible and the reason southern Gallet can feed itself.`,

  'splitneck-sea': `The sea between Gallet and the ice of Bjarl, narrow and cold. It is the last water before the southern tundra and the shortest crossing to it, which makes it the route for anything the Bjarl regions want from the north.`,

  'asp-river': `A river winding west across southern Gallet to the sea, drawn on the plate with every bend it has. It waters the plain between the Fused Lands and the delta and is fordable along most of its length, which makes it a boundary rather than a barrier.`,

  'fasting-veldt': `Dry ground at Gallet’s south-eastern corner, where the plain runs out before the ice begins. Nothing grazes here for long, and the name is advice rather than description.`,

  // --- Andtor -------------------------------------------------------------
  andtor: `The south-eastern region: jungle in the north, plains in the middle, ice and floes along its southern coast. It is the most varied ground on the plate and the least governed, with the Greatpeaks along its eastern edge and the Krakensea beyond them.

Andtor is where Ghur keeps the things that were already old when the realms were new. The jungles around Mektopsar and the Draconith Graveyard are not frontier country; they are somebody else’s country, and the settlements on the plate sit around their edges.`,

  'crawling-city': `A city that does not stay put — carried on the back of something that walks, so that the whole population moves with whatever it is riding. Nothing on this map is harder to besiege and nothing is harder to find twice.

It is drawn here on the northern Andtor plain above the Crawling Pits, because that is the country it works, but the position is a season’s claim rather than a location. An army that means to fight it has to catch it first.`,

  'crawling-pits-of-gharrentia': `A field of pits in northern Andtor where the ground has been undermined from below until it collapsed in a pattern. It is impassable to formed troops and perfectly passable to whatever dug it.`,

  'koatls-gullet': `A gorge at the edge of the Andtor jungle, narrow and deep, carrying the traffic between the plain and the trees. It is the front door to the jungle and the only one wide enough for an army.`,

  'primeval-jungle': `Old forest in northern Andtor, drawn on the plate as its own kind of terrain. It is dense enough to swallow a column, hot enough to halve one, and it has been there longer than anything else on this map.`,

  mektopsar: `A stone city in the Andtor jungle, built on a scale and in a style that has nothing to do with the settlements north of it. The plains around it carry its name, which is the map’s way of saying the city was here first and the geography was named after.

It is the one place in Andtor with real walls. Whatever holds it holds the jungle’s eastern approaches and the plains behind them.`,

  'underchasm-gulch': `A gulch in western Andtor where the ground has dropped away along a fault. It runs for miles, it is crossed in very few places, and it separates the Gallet border country from the Andtor plain.`,

  'lake-hoarfrost': `A cold lake in western Andtor, iced at the edges most of the year. It is the water supply for the whole western plain, which makes it the one fixed point in country that otherwise offers nothing to hold.`,

  'plains-of-mektopsar': `The open ground east of the jungle, named for the city that dominates it. It is the best marching country in Andtor and the ground any campaign against Mektopsar has to cross.`,

  'brokenjare-mawpath': `The mawpath running east across the Andtor plains toward the Greatpeaks. It links the jungle country to the eastern uplands and carries everything too large to go around.`,

  'ravening-rivers': `A braid of fast rivers cutting the eastern Andtor plain, changing course often enough that the plate draws the whole system rather than any one channel. Crossing them is a matter of timing rather than engineering.`,

  moulderpit: `A marked battle-site on the eastern Andtor plain, in the sour ground the rivers leave behind. Crossed weapons, a name, and nothing else.`,

  'bantus-gate': `A standing gate on the southern Andtor plain, drawn as a structure rather than a settlement — an arch on open ground with no walls attached to it. Whatever it opens onto, it is a fixed point in country that has very few, and armies orient on it.`,

  rimelake: `A settlement on the frozen southern edge of the Andtor plain, beside the lake that names it. It is the last permanent habitation before the floes and the staging post for anything working the southern ice.`,

  'shatterland-floes': `The broken ice along Andtor’s southern shore, neither land nor water for most of the year. It is passable on foot in the cold months and impassable to everything in the thaw, which makes the southern campaigning season very short.`,

  icebrow: `A settlement on the Andtor ice south-east of Rimelake, sited where the floes meet solid ground. It is a hunting station more than a town and it is the furthest south anything on this side of the map is permanently held.`,

  'greatpeaks-of-andtor': `The mountain range along Andtor’s eastern edge, the highest ground on the plate. It divides the Andtor plains from the Krakensea coast and it is crossed by nothing that does not have a mawpath to follow.`,

  'draconith-graveyard': `A field of draconith bones in eastern Andtor, large enough and old enough to be charted as terrain. The bones are the best building material in the region and the worst place in it to dig.`,

  harkanib: `A settlement at the eastern limit of the plate, on the Bloodclot coast where the map runs out mid-name. It is the last charted habitation in that direction and the point at which the Heartlands stop being the Heartlands.`,

  'bloodclot-coast': `The far eastern shore below Harkanib, red-stained and ironbound. It faces the open water beyond the map and takes the weather that comes off it.`,

  'donsian-reclaim': `Ground in south-eastern Andtor that someone took back — the plate names the act, not the claimant. It sits between the Draconith Graveyard and the Krakensea coast, on the only decent going between the two.`,

  'the-plummet': `A marked site on the Andtor coast above the Krakensea, where the land ends abruptly rather than sloping into the water. It is a landmark from the sea and a dead end from the land.`,

  'crumbling-coast': `The southern Andtor shore, actively falling into the Krakensea. Charts of it are out of date as a matter of course, and the settlements that were on it are not.`,

  'the-prongs': `A rank of ice-bound islets in the water between Andtor and Bjarl, standing out of the floes like teeth. They are a hazard to shipping and, when the ice binds, a causeway.`,

  // --- The Krakensea ------------------------------------------------------
  krakensea: `The eastern ocean, off the Andtor coast and beyond the Greatpeaks. It is deep, cold and named for what is in it, and the trade that crosses it does so hugging the shore.`,

  greedmouth: `A whirl in the Krakensea off the Andtor coast, drawn on the plate as a fixed feature because it is one. Ships give it a wide berth and the coast behind it is the better for being hard to reach.`,

  'coast-of-spines': `The Krakensea shore below Greedmouth, spined with rock. There is no landing along it, which makes the whole of southern Andtor approachable only from inland.`,

  // --- Bjarl and the frozen south -----------------------------------------
  bjarl: `The southern ice: a vast white region of fiords, floes and frozen plain running the width of the map below the Sea of Serpents and the Splitneck Sea. It is the largest region on the plate and the emptiest.

Bjarl is not lifeless, it is unpopulated, which is a different problem. The settlements on it — Coldfort, Vennbruck, Ehvell Ingate, Fierce Hope — are a day’s march from nothing, and every campaign fought here is fought against the region first and the enemy second.`,

  'grinding-vaults': `Ice caverns at Bjarl’s north-western edge, opening and closing with the movement of the sheet above them. They are shelter, they are a route under ground that cannot be crossed over it, and they are not reliably either.`,

  'bloodwhiten-fiords': `A fiord system on the northern Bjarl coast, cut deep enough to take ships a long way inland. It is the best natural harbour on the region’s north shore and the way an army gets into Bjarl without crossing it.`,

  'the-gnawing-fiords': `The eastern fiords of northern Bjarl, narrower and more broken than the Bloodwhiten. They carry the traffic from the Splitneck Sea into the interior and they freeze first.`,

  'everwinters-claw': `A hooked spur of ice and rock at the north-eastern corner of Bjarl, reaching toward the Andtor coast. It is the landward end of the crossing that the Prongs complete.`,

  'bjarl-throat': `The narrows where the northern ice gives onto the Bjarl interior — the region’s front door. Everything entering Bjarl from the north funnels through it, which makes it the single most defensible line on the southern half of the map.`,

  'harassic-cliffs': `An ice cliff wall across northern Bjarl, unclimbable along most of its length. It is the reason traffic into the region goes through the Throat and not over the top.`,

  'colossal-bite': `A marked site in northern Bjarl where a piece has been taken out of the ice sheet on a scale nothing living should manage. It is drawn as a feature, which means it has not filled in.`,

  'frostmaw-reach': `The stretch of frozen ground between the Harassic cliffs and the interior, open, level and completely exposed. An army crossing it is visible for a day in every direction and has nowhere to be if the weather turns.`,

  'ramhuts-spine': `A ridge running across northern Bjarl, the only high ground between the fiords and the interior. It shelters the country south of it and is the best observation post in the region.`,

  coldfort: `A fortress in northern Bjarl, sited where the routes from the fiords come together. It is small, it is stocked, and it is the only garrison between the coast and the interior — which means every campaign in northern Bjarl is about Coldfort whether the commanders intend it or not.`,

  'fractured-claw': `Broken ice at Bjarl’s north-western corner, shattered into a field of blocks and leads. It is impassable to anything wheeled and dangerous to everything else.`,

  'lookaway-point': `A marked point on Bjarl’s north-eastern shore, facing the Krakensea. The name reads as a sailors’ instruction, and it has been kept on the chart.`,

  'screaming-gate': `A gap in the northern Bjarl ice where the wind is funnelled and does what the name says. It is a route through the cliffs and an unpleasant one, and it is held for the same reason the Throat is.`,

  'ruins-of-ventivia': `A dead settlement in northern Bjarl, the only masonry in that quarter of the region. It has walls, it has cellars, and it is the obvious winter position for anything campaigning on the northern ice.`,

  'brutal-jut': `A rock spur standing out of the Bjarl ice at the head of the Tsilurian coast. It is a landmark, a shelter and the only fixed point on that shore.`,

  'red-delta': `A stained outwash fan on the central Bjarl plain where the meltwater comes down off the interior. The colour is in the sediment, and the plate does not explain where the sediment comes from.`,

  'stonejaw-flats': `Level frozen ground in central Bjarl, studded with the rock that gives it its name. It is the best marching country in the region and the worst place in it to be caught in the open.`,

  'corpse-lakes': `A chain of lakes in central Bjarl, frozen most of the year and named for what surfaces when they are not. They are the water supply for the interior and nobody drinks from them by choice.`,

  'war-of-basilisks': `A battle-site on the central Bjarl plain, marked with crossed weapons. The name says who fought; the plate does not say on whose behalf.`,

  'ehvell-ingate': `A settlement on the Tsilurian coast of Bjarl, sited at the point where the shore route turns inland. It is a gate in the sense that everything moving along that coast passes through it, and it takes its living accordingly.`,

  'tsilurian-coast': `The northern shore of southern Bjarl, running east from Ehvell Ingate. It is the only coast in the region that can be marched along, which makes it the region’s road.`,

  'monstrum-gougelands': `Torn country between the Sea of Serpents and the Bjarl ice, gouged into ridges and furrows on a scale that rules out weather. It is the worst going on this half of the map and it is why the southern crossing is made by sea.`,

  'fierce-hope': `A settlement on the southern shore of the Sea of Serpents, at the western edge of the Bjarl ice. It is the westernmost permanent holding in the region and it is named by people who knew what they were taking on.`,

  'ur-luparls-shellpeaks': `A range of shell-forms in southern Bjarl, standing out of the ice like a mountain chain and hollow enough to shelter in. They are the only cover between the Tsilurian coast and the southern fjords.`,

  'doppelgang-fjords': `Fjords on Bjarl’s southern coast, so alike in shape that the plate bothers to say so. Navigating them by eye is how ships end up in the wrong one, and the wrong one is a dead end.`,

  deathgorge: `A gorge in central Bjarl cut down through the ice into the rock beneath. It is a route through country that otherwise has none, and it is named for the price of using it.`,

  'ymnogs-trample-south': `The southern half of the great mawpath, crossing the Bjarl ice from the northern floes down toward the Winterbleed. That anything migrates this far south at all is the most remarkable thing on this part of the plate.`,

  vennbruck: `A settlement on the northern edge of southern Bjarl, beside the water the Veins drain into. It is the only town between Coldfort and the eastern lakes and the last stop before the interior.`,

  'the-veins': `A network of meltwater channels running across eastern Bjarl, drawn on the plate as a system rather than as rivers. They are the region’s drainage and, frozen, its footpaths.`,

  fangsgirdle: `A ring of ice teeth in eastern Bjarl, encircling the ground inside it. What it is girdling the plate does not say, and the circuit is complete enough that getting in means going over.`,

  'lake-maw': `A lake in eastern Bjarl, open water in a landscape that is otherwise frozen solid. Whatever keeps it liquid also keeps it worth reaching, and it is the only reliable water in that quarter.`,

  'drakbiter-mawpath': `A mawpath crossing southern Bjarl, beaten through the ice toward the Fangathrak walk. It is the only maintained line across that country and it is maintained by use rather than by anyone.`,

  'walk-of-fangathrak': `A route across southern Bjarl running down toward the Coarse Splint, named for whatever walks it. It is a track in the sense that the ground remembers being trodden, and it is the safest way south for that reason alone.`,

  'coarse-splint': `A splintered rock ridge at Bjarl’s south-western corner, the last hard ground before the map ends. It is shelter from the southern wind and there is nothing else on that coast that is.`,

  'the-winterbleed': `A stretch of southern Bjarl where the ice sheet is bleeding meltwater year-round, turning the ground to a slush that will not freeze and will not drain. It is impassable to formed troops in any season.`,

  'lake-gnasht': `A large lake in southern Bjarl, frozen at the edges and never entirely at the centre. It is the water the whole southern interior works around and the only reason anything lives there.`,

  // --- The Drontal tundra -------------------------------------------------
  'ceaim-of-ghuri-xza': `Claimed ground on the Drontal tundra, staked and named and marked on the realm map — which means the claim was made by something with the strength to have it recorded. What is being claimed is empty tundra, and the claim is the only thing on it.`,

  'drakmasher-mawpath': `The mawpath running east across the Drontal tundra toward the coast. It is the only named route across that country and it carries whatever crosses it.`,

  'bitinghollow-lakes': `A chain of hollow-set lakes on the eastern tundra, sunk below the level of the plain. They are sheltered, they are the water supply for the east, and the hollows are the only ground on the tundra that is out of the wind.`,

  'gruesome-heights': `High broken ground on the western Drontal tundra, standing over the ice around it. The heights are the only relief on that plain and the only position in it — an army that wants to hold the eastern tundra holds this or holds nothing.`,

  'the-ivory-citadel': `A fortress of bone on the Drontal tundra, raised where there is no stone to build with and no timber to burn. It is the one permanent strongpoint in the far south-east, and everything within a week’s march of it is either supplying it or besieging it.`,

  'ghurish-necrosia': `Dead ground south of the Ivory Citadel where the tundra has gone sour on a scale worth charting. Nothing grazes across it and nothing digs into it, and the plate gives it a name instead of an explanation.`,

  'icebear-prowl': `Hunting ground on the eastern tundra, named for what does the hunting. It is crossed by the caravans between the citadel and the coast, and it is crossed quickly.`,

  'drontal-tundra': `The frozen plain at the south-eastern corner of the plate: flat, bare, wind-scoured and enormous. It is the least contested ground on the map and would stay that way if the Ivory Citadel were not standing in the middle of it.`,

  'lake-grask': `A lake on the western Drontal tundra below the Ivory Citadel, iced over for most of the year. It is the citadel’s water and the reason the citadel is where it is.`,

  'lake-krakjaw': `A lake on the southern tundra, larger than Grask and further from anything. What is in it has kept the shore unsettled, and the plate names the lake rather than any place on it.`,
};
